A||||20||SteveJ||What's the best way to move a Fiero?||||5||
Z||000000||SteveJ||02-18-2001||11:01 AM||||Say you buy car in another state. How do you move it home? Can you rent a car trailer? From whom and what is it called in their business? ||||reg||5
Z||000001||2xsess||02-18-2001||11:22 AM||||You can rent a trailer or tow dolly from U-Haul.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>AOL IM : Only2xsess<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2xsess.gif">      <BR> <A HREF="http://kresgeus.homestead.com/FieroPage.html" TARGET=_blank>Visit My Website</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Hank is Here||02-18-2001||11:27 AM||||Why not just drive the car back?  Take a friend on a ride there or greyhound it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||SteveJ||02-18-2001||11:37 AM||||Ah, tow dolly. That might be the term I'm looking for. Seems most cars today have some mechanical problem or sell for big bucks. I just want to get one from someplace where they aren't all rotted from salt.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||02-18-2001||01:28 PM||||If you rent a trailer, as in putting the whole car on a trailer not just back wheels, make sure you put in on the trailer backwards. Put the engine tword the front of the trailer. We were towing my 85 across town and had the engine at the rear of the trailer. It started boucing and picked the whole back of my Bronco off the ground. Not a good thing to do.||||reg||1
Z||000005||tgowens||02-18-2001||01:38 PM||||seanh, that's a good idea except for the fact that, if you have to load the car on a trailer with ramps you need to go up nose first, otherwise you will rip the front fascia off (especially if it has the aero nose on it) - cars we tow here at the shop we move on a trailer with a winch and believe me, there is nothing like watching a front end being ripped off when loading on a trailer (we did that to a few of our parts cars we moved them)||||reg||1
Z||000006||SteveJ||02-18-2001||03:04 PM||||So if you use a tow dolly and the car is manual no problem. Put the front up on the tow and put it in neutral. But if its an auto we gotta put the back up on the tow dolly, right? Facia problems now? . . .and I'm new to this but with the front end on the ground being towed doesn't the car wander? I would imagine that even if the stering wheel was tied down it would still wander enough to be anoying at expressway speeds. Anybody know about this?<p>[This message has been edited by SteveJ (edited 02-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Hank is Here||02-18-2001||03:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by SteveJ:<BR><B>I'm new to this but with the front end on the ground being towed doesn't the car wander? I would imagine that even if the stering wheel was tied down it would still wander enough to be anoying at expressway speeds. Anybody know about this?<BR>(edited 02-18-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This depends on speed.  For highway use it probably would mean massive wander.  You should leave the steering wheel locked then also tie it down another way too.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfiero||02-18-2001||04:35 PM||||My personal opinion is if you use a tow dolly always tow it backwards (rear wheels on dolly).  That centers the weight much better and your tow car won't be all over the road.<P>I have towed many Fieros (with a Fiero) this way without any problems......most were cross country trips (2000+ miles).<P>SteveJ,<P>The Fiero will wander more with the rear wheels on the ground than with the front (since most of the weight is in the rear).  With the steering wheel locked it will not wander......Like I said above, I have towed many Fieros cross country this way and I could cruise at 70-80MPH with no problems at all.  Regardless if it is a manual or auto. always put the rear wheels on the dolly.||||reg||1
Z||000009||tgowens||02-18-2001||06:45 PM||||Mike (mrfiero) is right on the use of the tow dolly and especially by towing with a Fiero - the only concern when towing backwards is the height of the dolly and the hitch.........if it sits low to the ground (as in towing with a Fiero) then you should have no problems.||||reg||1
Z||000010||SteveJ||02-18-2001||06:58 PM||||mffiero you got a hitch on a Fiero! I am amazed. You own the dolly or rent it?||||reg||1
Z||000011||mrfiero||02-18-2001||08:03 PM||||Yes I do.  It's on an auto. '88 GT.  I have sold it to a friend, but I still borrow it from time to time when I need to tow something.<P>I bought the set-up from the Fiero Store.  I would advise against using a nice Fiero for a tow vehicle......it really gets abused.  I would also recommend using a Fiero with an auto........you would probably burn up a lot of clutches while towing.  The GT I use has almost 140K miles and seems to like towing.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Make sure your brakes are up to the task, as well as the cooling system......both will get hot <P>I don't own a dolly, but I have a friend who has one.  I use it whenever I need to tow something.<P><BR>Let me say this though.......if you have access to a truck then use it instead of a Fiero.  If you do use a Fiero make sure you always leave plenty of room between you and the car ahead of you......there are no such things as quick stops, you must plan your stops ahead of time.  I usually down shift it manually to low 2 (engine braking) then to low 1 while applying the brakes......this realy helps it slow down and helps keep the brakes cooler (since you're not relying on them 100%).<P>Enough blabbing.....the important thing is to tow a Fiero backwards when using a dolly.<P>E-mail me privately if you want some pointers when using a Fiero as a tow vehicle.     ||||reg||10
Z||000012||SteveJ||02-19-2001||09:30 AM||||Yea FieroStore, I get their catalog. Trailer hitch on a Fiero was such a crazy  idea I turned the page. Once I was blind but now I see. Here it is. . .hum. . .||||reg||1
Z||000013||Phil||02-19-2001||09:49 AM||||I recently rented a car caddy trailer from U Haul and transported an 88 coupe. U-Haul said the GT wouldn't fit but the coupe would. I loaded it nose first and no trouble with hitting the front and it towed quite well 65-70 mph. Tow vechile was an S10 pickup||||reg||1
Z||000014||SteveJ||02-19-2001||10:11 AM||||Don't have anybody here with any truck. Other car is my wife's Deville which is so damned fragile I'm afraid to use it. Shame about those cars. $40,000 they want for 'em and they break constantly. I'll never have another. There is an '88 GT about a mile from me with a flat tire and the fall leaves around it. Right side vent over the battery is off too. Spring might just produce a for sale sign on that one.||||reg||1
Z||000015||DRH||02-19-2001||02:00 PM||||Stop by and ask before the for sale sign goes up. Offer to take it off their hands... you never know. Sometimes one man's junk is another man's treasure.||||reg||3
Z||000016||gabriel||02-19-2001||04:09 PM||||Steve:<P>   How far away is this car, anyway?<BR>Long distance w/ a dolly is doable, but I don't like it for a lot of reasons.<P>   If you are going to rent a dolly from U-Haul, you may as well just go ahead & rent the AT. (Auto Transporter)  It's not much different in price and it resolves all the above issues/concerns & keeps the mileage off of your new Fiero.  Just call a full service U-Haul in your area or the 800#.<P>  Sometimes it takes them a couple of days to get one for you.<P>  Otherwise, I sometimes move the Fiero interstate on a transporter for others at about half the commerical price.<BR>   Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Phil||02-19-2001||04:24 PM||||There is one downside from U-Haul, at first they weren't going to rent me a trailer because I didn't have a full side truck although I did have a class III hitch. They said they could only rent me a dolly and I said OK, a day later they called and said they didn't have a dolly in the area(I was renting one way)but they would let me have a trailer for the same price. Some of the major rental co.s won't rent you a trailer with out renting you a truck also. Best bet is to find a small local  rental co. as they tend to have less stringent  regulations. I did tow with a dolly once(rear wheels on dolly) and I did pick up a wiggle at about 60 mph but the car does have bad front control arms.<BR>This post most likely has no useful information for you but it does keep my post numbers up.||||reg||1
Z||000018||SteveJ||02-19-2001||04:48 PM||||What do you mean your post does no good? A chorus of people saying they have done it and how is educating and enabling.||||reg||1
Z||000019||danny boy||02-19-2001||05:22 PM|||| I put an '85 GT in the BED of my dad's fullsize Chevy truck and hauled it.Ok, so it was a parts car. and it was stripped. and it was cut in half. oh well.<BR> I always use a trailer when I tow cars, and I make sure the trailer i rent has its own brakes. I don't know if tow dollys are made with electric brakes like trailers are, but it is worth checking into.||||reg||1
Z||000020||RossT||02-20-2001||01:24 AM||||I have rented u-haul trailors twice to tow fiero cross country. The u-hauls are very heavy (2300-2500lbs), and you will need a good setup to pull it. On one of the rentals, you couldn't open the fiero car door, even with the the trailors tire gard in the down postition.  Thus you might need tools to remove the guard so you can get in and out of the fiero.  I have pictures of a fiero on a u-haul if you want them.<P>U-haul has a great web site with price quotes online..||||reg||1
